## Mathematical content (independently reproduced by Lead + verified by critic)
**Upper bound (area/cheapest-semiperimeter argument).** Let a,b be the integer sides, s=a+b (perimeter 2s). Distinct perimeters ⇔ distinct s. For fixed s, area ab is minimized at an endpoint: cost(s)=s−1 for 2≤s≤501 (1×(s−1)) and cost(s)=500(s−500) for 502≤s≤1000 (500×(s−500)). Since cost is nondecreasing,
250000 = Σ area ≥ Σ cost(sᵢ) ≥ Σ_{s=2}^{k+1} cost(s).
Σ_{s=2}^{521}cost(s) = 125250 + 115000 = **240250 ≤ 250000**, while Σ_{s=2}^{522}cost(s) = 240250 + 11000 = **251250 > 250000**. Hence **k ≤ 520**.

**Matching construction (explicitly verified; 520 rectangles, semiperimeters exactly {2,…,521}).**
- Bottom half y∈[0,250]: for i=1..249 the two unit strips [0,i]×[i−1,i] and [i,500]×[i−1,i], plus [0,500]×[249,250] → 499 strips (s = 2..250 and 252..501, plus 501).
- Top half y∈[250,500]: the bar [499,500]×[250,500] (1×250, s=251) and strips [0,499]×[y,y+t] for t=3..22 (heights sum 250, s=499+t=502..521).
- Count 520; total area 250000; all sides integers; all perimeters distinct. Verified independently (occupancy grid / all 134,940 pairs) by solver-6, by the critic, and by the Lead. `run_python` verification returned: area 250000, disjoint/covered True, s-set exactly {2,…,521}.

**Small-n cross-check:** f(1..8)=1,1,3,4,6,7,8,9, confirming the min-area bound is tight (note f(2)=1, correcting the anchor in the task text).

**Orientation:** in any finite tiling of a rectangle by rectangles, all tiles are axis-parallel (smallest nonzero rotation angle argument), so the distinction is vacuous; the non-axis-parallel relaxation would only give k ≤ 706.

